BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Livestream Viewing: Japanese Canadian Monument Opening Ceremony
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T170000
UID:d7f7e3ac5b11cca3d5a548e3700b751a@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Announcement for unveiling of the Japanese Canadian Monument *
 Momiji Capacity has been reached\, it is now waitlist only! Please join us
  at the Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre (JCCC) or Momiji Health Care Soc
 iety (Momiji) on Saturday\, October 3\, 2026 at 3:30pm EST for the live st
 reaming of the opening ceremony of the Japanese Canadian Monument that is 
 taking place in Victoria\, British Columbia. A pre-show program will begin
  at 3:30pm\, and the opening ceremony livestream will begin at 4:00pm. Fur
 ther information per livestream location is outlined below. The Japanese C
 anadian Monument lists the names of all Japanese Canadians uprooted in 194
 2 AND a second set of names of children born after uprooting and up to and
  including March 31\, 1949\, after which freedom to return to the coast wa
 s fully granted. The monument acknowledges the forced dispossession and in
 ternment of the community during World War II while celebrating their resi
 lience and impact on the region. These names and locations engraved on the
  wall provide a stark illustration of a community torn apart by policies t
 hat were driven by a confluence of race\, economics\, and power. To set th
 em in stone here is to ensure they are never again forgotten or marginaliz
 ed. Please register from the button below to attend the Japanese Canadian 
 Monument opening ceremony livestream: Register Light refreshments will be 
 served\, and a souvenir book will be gifted to all attendees. Please note 
 that capacity will be limited based on location selected ( choice between 
 JCCC and Momiji (Waitlist Only) ) . Registration will open at 2:00pm EST o
 n August 24\, 2026 and will close at 11:00am EST on September 24\, 2026 or
  once capacity at each location is reached\, whichever comes first. For mo
 re information\, please refer to https://hcmc.uvic.ca/project/monument/ . 
 For any questions\, please reach out to jccc@jccc.on.ca . Livestream Event
  Information at the JCCC: Thank you for joining the JCCC at the monument u
 nveiling livestream on Saturday\, October 3\, 2026! Doors will open at 2:3
 0pm EST on October 3\, 2026 for sign in\, and the pre-show program and liv
 estream will begin at 3:30pm EST in Kobayashi Hall. Light refreshments wil
 l be served\, and a souvenir book will be gifted to all attendees. Please 
 note that the maximum capacity of the livestream event in Kobayashi Hall i
 s 300 guests. Once the registration form reaches maximum capacity\, a wait
 list for 50 guests will be monitored. Registrants will be contacted via em
 ail in September to confirm their entrance status (enrolled or waitlist) .
  Further details will be communicated via email\, e-blast\, and social med
 ia in September. The JCCC will also be replaying the livestream throughout
  the evening beginning at 7:00pm EST on October 3\, 2026 during Nuit Blanc
 he\, as well as during Issei Day on October 4\, 2026. Registration is not 
 required for Nuit Blanche livestream viewing on October 3\, 2026. To regis
 ter for Issei Day\, please visit: Issei Day page . A souvenir book will al
 so be gifted to all Issei Day attendees on October 4\, 2026. We look forwa
 rd to seeing you there! Livestream Event Information at Momiji Health Care
  Society: *Momiji Capacity has been reached\, now waitlist only! Momiji wo
 uld like to welcome our community to watch the livestream of the monument 
 unveiling in Victoria\, BC\, from the comfort of the Yone and Coby Kobayas
 hi Auditorium at Momiji Seniors Residence. Momiji will be providing a smal
 l\, boxed meal for the first 150 community members and 70 tenants register
 ed. Once we hit capacity\, a waitlist will be created\, and we will be wor
 king with the JCCC to ensure that as many people as possible are able to w
 atch the event. Since the event is overlapping with our annual bazaar\, we
  are inviting attendees to arrive anytime from Noon to 3:00pm and support 
 our annual bazaar before joining the livestream. Further details will be c
 ommunicated by email and social media in September.\n\nCategories: Communi
 ty Event
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/livestream-viewing-japanese-canadian-
 monument-opening-ceremony
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Paradise
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270103T190000
UID:43283f91f18b34c68a346ddc96e9bd68@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:The JCCC Gallery's fall exhibition is Paradise : a multimedia 
 installation by Japanese Canadian artists and longtime family friends Mitc
 hell Akiyama and Emma Nishimura that explores intergenerational inheritanc
 e. Combining print\, video\, and sculpture\, the work reflects on their re
 lationship to the legacy of Japanese Canadian internment experienced by th
 eir paternal grandparents. The exhibition draws on a shared childhood memo
 ry of a summer day in Northern Ontario\, where the artists and their sibli
 ngs built a temporary “Frog Paradise.” This moment of play\, shaped by
  a sense of safety and belonging\, becomes a lens for considering how one 
 group’s utopia can exist alongside another’s harm. The installation in
 cludes video that intercuts footage of this experience with recent visits 
 to former internment sites in British Columbia. Bridging personal narrativ
 e and historical context\, Paradise examines the forced relocation and inc
 arceration of Japanese Canadians\, tracing how this legacy continues to sh
 ape memory\, displacement\, and belonging across generations. Please join 
 us for our opening reception on October 25 th from 2 to 4 pm Opening Recep
 tion More programming to be announced shortly!\n\nCategories: Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/paradise
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Issei Day 2026
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T163000
UID:2945d1ce0810dfd7afca298b50932ffb@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:A Tribute to the Seniors of our Community The Board of Directo
 rs of the Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre extends an invitation to Issei
  Day on Sunday\, October 4\, 2026 \, and would like to welcome all who are
  70 years of age and older. This year will be the 64th year of celebrating
  Issei Day! We are so excited to welcome you back with a day full of activ
 ities\, with entertainment of songs and odori. As usual there will be a wo
 nderful and tasty bento and sweets served during intermission. To accommod
 ate everyone with seating and a bento\, we require that everyone pre-regis
 ter. Registration will open on Tuesday\, September 1 and the deadline will
  be Tuesday\, September 22 . It is wonderful to see so many of our seniors
  come to this event but there is a maximum capacity to ensure everyone can
  sit\, relax and enjoy the afternoon\, so don’t delay - register today! 
 We look forward to seeing you. Date: Sunday\, October 4\, 2026 Time: 2pm -
  4:30pm Venue: Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre (6 Sakura Way) Registrati
 on: Tuesday\, September 1 - Tuesday\, September 22\, 2026 Register\n\nCate
 gories: Community Event
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/issei-day-2026
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Paradise Opening Night Reception
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261025T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261025T160000
UID:a6e12617a0181c2dece986440238c643@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:In the multimedia exhibit\, Paradise \, which includes sculptu
 re\, print\, and video\, Japanese Canadian artists and childhood friends E
 mma Nishimura and Mitchell Akiyama grapple with their family histories of 
 internment. They consider how utopian worlds are created through resilienc
 e and displacement\, while urging us to remember that utopia for some may 
 not be utopia for all. Find out more about the exhibit here . Please join 
 us at our opening reception for a chance to take in this meaningful show a
 nd to meet the artists! Light refreshments will be served.\n\nCategories: 
 Community Event\, Social\, Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/paradise-opening-night-reception
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Ishizue Award Dinner 2026
